\lessonlabel{3}
\section{Planning and Decision‑Making}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item define what a plan is and why planning matters;
  \item list the main steps of a systematic planning process;
  \item apply a simple decision‑making method to choose between alternatives;
  \item recognise common pitfalls in planning and decision‑making.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{What is planning?}

\term{Planning} is the activity of setting goals, deciding what actions are needed to reach them, and arranging those actions in time. A plan turns an abstract aim (e.g., “finish a project”) into concrete tasks (e.g., “write outline”, “collect data”, “draft report”) with deadlines.

\begin{example}
A student wants to complete a semester project in 12 weeks. The plan might list: week 1–2 literature review, week 3–4 data collection, week 5–6 analysis, week 7–8 write results, week 9–10 draft discussion, week 11 revise, week 12 submit.
\end{example}

\subsection{Steps of a systematic planning process}

A typical planning cycle contains four stages:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \term{Goal definition.} State the desired outcome clearly and measurably.
  \item \term{Task identification.} Break the goal into smaller, manageable tasks.
  \item \term{Scheduling.} Assign each task a start date, duration, and deadline.
  \item \term{Resource allocation.} Decide which people, tools, or funds are needed for each task.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{example}
For a group presentation, the goal is “deliver a 15‑minute talk with visual slides”. Tasks: research topic, create slides, rehearse, set up equipment. Scheduling: research (days 1‑3), slides (days 4‑6), rehearsal (day 7), equipment check (day 8). Resources: two researchers, a laptop, projector.
\end{example}

\subsection{Simple decision‑making method: weighted scoring}

When several alternatives exist, the \term{weighted scoring} method helps choose the best one.

\begin{enumerate}
  \item List criteria that matter (e.g., cost, time, quality).
  \item Assign a weight to each criterion (total 100 %).
  \item Score each alternative on each criterion (0–10).
  \item Multiply scores by weights and sum for each alternative.
  \item Choose the alternative with the highest total.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{example}
A student must pick a software tool for data analysis. Criteria: cost (30 %), ease of use (40 %), features (30 %). Alternatives:

\begin{tabular}{lccc}
 & Cost & Ease & Features \\
Tool A & 8 & 6 & 9 \\
Tool B & 5 & 9 & 7 \\
\end{tabular}

Weighted totals:
Tool A = $8\!\times\!0.30 + 6\!\times\!0.40 + 9\!\times\!0.30 = 7.3$  
Tool B = $5\!\times\!0.30 + 9\!\times\!0.40 + 7\!\times\!0.30 = 7.0$

Tool A scores slightly higher, so it is the preferred choice.
\end{example}

\subsection{Common pitfalls}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Over‑optimism.} Assuming tasks will finish faster than realistic.
  \item \term{Scope creep.} Adding new tasks without adjusting time or resources.
  \item \term{Ignoring constraints.} Forgetting budget limits or availability of team members.
  \item \term{Lack of review.} Not checking progress against the plan, leading to hidden delays.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}
A project team schedules a prototype build for week 3 but later discovers that a key component will not be delivered until week 4. Because the plan was not reviewed, the whole timeline shifts, causing a missed deadline.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} For any upcoming activity ask: (1) Have I written down a clear goal? (2) Do I have a list of tasks, dates, and needed resources? (3) Have I compared alternatives with a systematic method? If any answer is no, the planning or decision step is incomplete.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Write a one‑sentence goal for a personal fitness routine you would like to start in the next month.
  \item Break that goal into at least four tasks and assign a realistic duration to each.
  \item Using weighted scoring, decide between two study locations (library vs. coffee shop). Choose criteria, weights, and scores, then compute the totals.
  \item Identify one potential pitfall in your plan from (2) and suggest a way to avoid it.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Planning & Organising goals, tasks, timing, and resources to achieve an outcome. \\
Goal definition & Stating a desired result in clear, measurable terms. \\
Weighted scoring & A decision‑making technique that rates alternatives against weighted criteria. \\
Scope creep & Uncontrolled expansion of project scope without adjusting resources or schedule. \\
Over‑optimism & Unrealistic expectation that tasks will take less time or effort than they actually require. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
